1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,299
Bitcoin moved while you were asleep.

2
00:00:02,299 --> 00:00:02,990
You wake up,

3
00:00:02,990 --> 00:00:04,217
and the number on your screen

4
00:00:04,217 --> 00:00:05,720
is a different number.

5
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:07,455
So who did that?

6
00:00:07,455 --> 00:00:09,071
There is no central bank here.

7
00:00:09,071 --> 00:00:11,194
No committee sets this price.

8
00:00:11,194 --> 00:00:13,686
And yet something clearly moved it.

9
00:00:13,686 --> 00:00:14,708
Stay to the end

10
00:00:14,708 --> 00:00:17,743
and you will know which forces move Bitcoin in hours,

11
00:00:17,743 --> 00:00:19,149
which move it in years,

12
00:00:19,149 --> 00:00:21,400
and the one number to watch instead.

13
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:23,896
Twenty-one million coins.

14
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:25,363
That is all there will ever be.

15
00:00:25,363 --> 00:00:26,925
It was written into the code

16
00:00:26,925 --> 00:00:28,767
before most of them existed.

17
00:00:28,767 --> 00:00:30,077
No committee can raise it.

18
00:00:30,077 --> 00:00:32,687
No emergency can print more.

19
00:00:32,687 --> 00:00:35,739
So where does a price come from?

20
00:00:35,739 --> 00:00:38,223
A price is not a fact about an object.

21
00:00:38,223 --> 00:00:40,674
It is an agreement between two people.

22
00:00:40,674 --> 00:00:42,413
One person willing to sell.

23
00:00:42,413 --> 00:00:43,668
One willing to buy.

24
00:00:43,668 --> 00:00:45,634
Where they meet is the price.

25
00:00:45,634 --> 00:00:47,374
So Bitcoin has two sides,

26
00:00:47,374 --> 00:00:49,620
and only one is up for grabs.

27
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:52,117
The supply side was settled in the code.

28
00:00:52,117 --> 00:00:53,913
New coins arrive on a schedule

29
00:00:53,913 --> 00:00:56,012
that only ever slows down.

30
00:00:56,012 --> 00:00:57,315
Fifty new coins a block.

31
00:00:57,315 --> 00:00:58,678
Then twenty-five.

32
00:00:58,678 --> 00:00:59,731
Then twelve and a half.

33
00:00:59,731 --> 00:01:01,243
Then six and a quarter.

34
00:01:01,243 --> 00:01:03,510
Now just over three.

35
00:01:03,510 --> 00:01:04,939
Every four years it halves,

36
00:01:04,939 --> 00:01:06,887
and nobody votes on it.

37
00:01:06,887 --> 00:01:08,721
So every argument about the price

38
00:01:08,721 --> 00:01:11,054
is an argument about demand.

39
00:01:11,054 --> 00:01:13,417
Who moves it in hours?

40
00:01:13,417 --> 00:01:14,997
Start with the whales.

41
00:01:14,997 --> 00:01:16,396
A whale is one wallet

42
00:01:16,396 --> 00:01:18,909
holding a thousand bitcoin or more.

43
00:01:18,909 --> 00:01:21,689
Their power is not the size of the pile.

44
00:01:21,689 --> 00:01:24,579
It is that almost none of it is for sale.

45
00:01:24,579 --> 00:01:28,077
Most bitcoin sits in wallets that have not moved in years.

46
00:01:28,077 --> 00:01:29,557
What is on an exchange today

47
00:01:29,557 --> 00:01:31,646
is a sliver of the total.

48
00:01:31,646 --> 00:01:32,852
So the order book is thin -

49
00:01:32,852 --> 00:01:33,804
just a queue of offers

50
00:01:33,804 --> 00:01:35,890
to buy and to sell.

51
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:37,794
A thin queue moves easily,

52
00:01:37,794 --> 00:01:38,639
and they have names

53
00:01:38,639 --> 00:01:40,640
for how that gets done.

54
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:41,441
A sell wall.

55
00:01:41,441 --> 00:01:42,204
Spoofing.

56
00:01:42,204 --> 00:01:44,064
A pump and a dump.

57
00:01:44,064 --> 00:01:46,192
But none of that is choosing the price.

58
00:01:46,192 --> 00:01:49,183
It is exploiting how few people are selling.

59
00:01:49,183 --> 00:01:51,485
And who moves it in years?

60
00:01:51,485 --> 00:01:53,929
Developers never touch the price.

61
00:01:53,929 --> 00:01:55,445
They maintain the protocol -

62
00:01:55,445 --> 00:01:58,758
the rules every computer on the network follows.

63
00:01:58,758 --> 00:02:00,661
SegWit, in twenty seventeen,

64
00:02:00,661 --> 00:02:03,462
changed how transactions pack into a block.

65
00:02:03,462 --> 00:02:05,498
It did not move the price that week.

66
00:02:05,498 --> 00:02:06,769
It decides whether the network

67
00:02:06,769 --> 00:02:09,374
is worth anything in ten years.

68
00:02:09,374 --> 00:02:11,490
Governments are the loud ones.

69
00:02:11,490 --> 00:02:14,329
China banned trading and mining in twenty twenty-one,

70
00:02:14,329 --> 00:02:15,861
and the market fell.

71
00:02:15,861 --> 00:02:17,138
The United States approved

72
00:02:17,138 --> 00:02:19,527
spot bitcoin exchange traded funds

73
00:02:19,527 --> 00:02:21,250
in early twenty twenty-four,

74
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:23,158
and it ran to new highs.

75
00:02:23,158 --> 00:02:25,922
So put the two speeds side by side.

76
00:02:25,922 --> 00:02:26,699
In hours.

77
00:02:26,699 --> 00:02:27,849
A whale's sell order.

78
00:02:27,849 --> 00:02:29,883
A regulator's announcement.

79
00:02:29,883 --> 00:02:31,497
A headline anyone can trade.

80
00:02:31,497 --> 00:02:33,701
Now the ones that work in years.

81
00:02:33,701 --> 00:02:35,362
A change to the protocol.

82
00:02:35,362 --> 00:02:36,885
Legal clarity, or none.

83
00:02:36,885 --> 00:02:39,409
Whether the thing still works.

84
00:02:39,409 --> 00:02:42,588
Nobody is driving

85
00:02:42,588 --> 00:02:43,831
Ask it properly.

86
00:02:43,831 --> 00:02:45,165
Who sets the price of bitcoin

87
00:02:45,165 --> 00:02:47,136
right now, this second?

88
00:02:47,136 --> 00:02:48,099
The biggest whale?

89
00:02:48,099 --> 00:02:49,104
The core developers?

90
00:02:49,104 --> 00:02:51,137
A regulator in Washington?

91
00:02:51,137 --> 00:02:55,261
Or the last person willing to sell?

92
00:02:55,261 --> 00:02:56,282
The price is whatever

93
00:02:56,282 --> 00:02:58,106
the next buyer and the next seller

94
00:02:58,106 --> 00:02:59,279
agree on.

95
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:01,903
Which is why size is not control.

96
00:03:01,903 --> 00:03:04,389
A whale holding a hundred thousand coins

97
00:03:04,389 --> 00:03:06,709
cannot sell them at today's price.

98
00:03:06,709 --> 00:03:07,848
The moment they start,

99
00:03:07,848 --> 00:03:10,215
it stops being today's price.

100
00:03:10,215 --> 00:03:12,644
There is a plainer way to say it.

101
00:03:12,644 --> 00:03:17,608
The price is set by the last person willing to sell.

102
00:03:17,608 --> 00:03:19,515
So who controls Bitcoin's price?

103
00:03:19,515 --> 00:03:20,476
Nobody does.

104
00:03:20,476 --> 00:03:22,219
Everybody does.

105
00:03:22,219 --> 00:03:24,796
So what should you watch?

106
00:03:24,796 --> 00:03:27,255
Which changes what is worth watching.

107
00:03:27,255 --> 00:03:28,788
Not the whale wallets.

108
00:03:28,788 --> 00:03:30,708
By the time a big transfer shows up,

109
00:03:30,708 --> 00:03:32,502
the trade is already done.

110
00:03:32,502 --> 00:03:33,537
Watch how much bitcoin

111
00:03:33,537 --> 00:03:35,919
is sitting on exchanges instead.

112
00:03:35,919 --> 00:03:37,851
Coins moving onto an exchange

113
00:03:37,851 --> 00:03:39,441
are getting ready to sell.

114
00:03:39,441 --> 00:03:40,381
Coins moving off

115
00:03:40,381 --> 00:03:42,054
are going to sleep.

116
00:03:42,054 --> 00:03:43,201
So - back to the morning

117
00:03:43,201 --> 00:03:45,296
you woke up to a different number.

118
00:03:45,296 --> 00:03:47,224
The question was never who moved it.

119
00:03:47,224 --> 00:03:50,084
It is how few people were left to buy.

120
00:03:50,084 --> 00:03:50,738
Know that,

121
00:03:50,738 --> 00:03:52,558
and it stops looking like a decision,

122
00:03:52,558 --> 00:03:53,984
and starts looking like a room

123
00:03:53,984 --> 00:03:56,367
changing its mind at once.

124
00:03:56,367 --> 00:03:58,262
The full breakdown is linked below,

125
00:03:58,262 --> 00:04:00,625
and nothing here is financial advice.

126
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:03,392
If that was useful, subscribe.
